STEP 2: Service Enumeration

Why: To find a service that allows remote login or remote command execution.

nmap -sV -p 21,22,23,80,443,445 10.10.55.X


Look for:

SSH (22)

FTP (21)

Telnet (23)

Web service (80/443)

STEP 3: Identify Vulnerable Login / RCE Service

Why: CEH typically uses weak credentials, misconfigured services, or known vulnerable apps.

Common exam scenarios:

SSH with weak credentials

FTP anonymous login

Web app with command execution

Vulnerable service detected via version

Optional check:

nmap --script vuln 10.10.55.X

STEP 4: Exploit Remote Login OR Command Execution
🔹 Option A: Remote Login (SSH / FTP)

Why: Direct shell access is easiest to retrieve files.

Example:

ssh user@10.10.55.X


If FTP:

ftp 10.10.55.X

🔹 Option B: Remote Command Execution (Web App)

Why: Some CEH labs expose command execution via web input.

Test payload:

; id
; whoami
; pwd


If command execution works, spawn a shell:

/bin/bash

STEP 5: Locate the Sensitive File

Why: The file location is usually unknown.

find / -name Netnormal.txt 2>/dev/null


Typical locations:

/home/user/

/var/www/

/tmp/

STEP 6: Read the File

Why: The content of this file is the final answer.

cat Netnormal.txt


✔ Copy the exact content
✔ Preserve case sensitivity
✔ No extra spaces or lines

